### Manual walkthrough of the session API (Days 15-16: cookie auth, CSRF, session revalidation, rate limiting).
### Requires: `docker compose up -d`, the API running on its "https" launch profile (the session and antiforgery
### cookies are Secure + __Host-, which a real browser — and the antiforgery system itself — only accepts over
### HTTPS), and staff seeded (`dotnet run --project src/TutoringCentre.Api -- seed`, with Seed:Password configured):
###   dotnet dev-certs https --trust   (once per machine)
###   dotnet run --project src/TutoringCentre.Api --launch-profile https
###
### The password is never written here. Create a git-ignored `.env` file at the repo root containing:
###   password=<the Seed:Password value you configured>
### VS Code's REST Client extension reads it via {{$dotenv password}}; no secret is ever committed.

@baseUrl = https://localhost:7197

### 0. Fetch a CSRF token — expect 200, Cache-Control: no-store, and the __Host-tcm.xsrf cookie.
### Every POST below sends it back as X-XSRF-TOKEN; re-run this step whenever a POST fails with 403 auth.csrf_invalid.
# @name antiforgery
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/antiforgery

### 1. Log in as owner@nile.test — expect 200, Set-Cookie __Host-tcm.session, activeCentreId = nile-centre's id
# @name loginOwner
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgery.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "email": "owner@nile.test",
  "password": "{{$dotenv password}}"
}

### 2. GET /api/me — expect 200, active centre nile-centre
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/me

### 3. Log in as teacher@both.test — expect 200, no active centre (two active memberships, no auto-select)
# @name loginTeacher
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgery.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "email": "teacher@both.test",
  "password": "{{$dotenv password}}"
}

### 4. GET /api/me — expect 200, activeCentreId null, two memberships.
### Copy the "maadi-hub" membership's centreId from the response body into @maadiHubCentreId below.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/me

### 5. Select maadi-hub — expect 204
@maadiHubCentreId = replace-with-the-centreId-from-step-4
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/session/centre
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgery.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "centreId": "{{maadiHubCentreId}}"
}

### 6. GET /api/me — expect 200, active centre maadi-hub
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/me

### 7. Select a random GUID — expect 403 tenant.no_membership; /api/me still shows maadi-hub afterwards
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/session/centre
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgery.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "centreId": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000"
}

### 8. Logout WITHOUT the CSRF header — expect 403 auth.csrf_invalid; the session still works afterwards
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/logout

### 8b. GET /api/me — expect 200 (step 8 had no side effects)
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/me

### 9. Logout, this time with the token — expect 204
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/logout
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgery.response.body.$.token}}

### 10. GET /api/me — expect 401
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/me

### Manual walkthrough of the subjects API (Day 26): the six Subject endpoints against a real session.

### 11. Fetch a fresh CSRF token (the session from step 9 ended) — expect 200
# @name antiforgerySubjects
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/antiforgery

### 12. Log in as owner@nile.test — expect 200, active centre nile-centre
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "email": "owner@nile.test",
  "password": "{{$dotenv password}}"
}

### 13. List subjects — expect 200, { "items": [ { "id", "name", "status", "version" } ] } for Nile only
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/subjects

### 14. Create a subject — expect 201, Location: /api/subjects/{newId}, body { "id" }
# @name createSubject
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/subjects
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "name": "Geography"
}

### 15. GET the created subject via the Location header — expect 200, name "Geography", status "active"
GET {{baseUrl}}{{createSubject.response.headers.Location}}

### 16. Rename it — copy the "id" from step 14 and the "version" from step 15 below first — expect 204
@subjectId = replace-with-the-id-from-step-14
@subjectVersion = replace-with-the-version-from-step-15
PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/subjects/{{subjectId}}
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "name": "World Geography",
  "version": {{subjectVersion}}
}

### 17. Archive it with the now-stale @subjectVersion from step 16 — expect 409 concurrency.stale.
### Re-run step 15 to read the current version, update @subjectVersion, then re-run this request — expect 204.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/subjects/{{subjectId}}/archive
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "version": {{subjectVersion}}
}

### 18. Restore it — re-run step 15 first to refresh @subjectVersion to the archived row's current version — expect 204
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/subjects/{{subjectId}}/restore
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "version": {{subjectVersion}}
}

### Manual walkthrough of the staff API and password change (Day 30), continuing owner@nile.test's session from step 12.

### 19. List staff — expect 200, { "items": [ { "membershipId", "userId", "displayName", "email", "role", "status", "version", "isCurrentUser" } ] }
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/staff

### 20. Create a staff member — expect 201, Cache-Control: no-store, body { "membershipId", "userId", "temporaryPassword" }
# @name createStaff
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/staff
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "email": "new-teacher@nile.test",
  "displayName": "New Teacher",
  "role": "teacher",
  "preferredLocale": "en"
}

### 21. Change their role — copy "membershipId" from step 20 and the new member's "version" from step 19 (re-run it) first — expect 204
@staffMembershipId = replace-with-the-membershipId-from-step-20
@staffVersion = replace-with-the-version-from-step-19-rerun
PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/staff/{{staffMembershipId}}/role
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "role": "secretary",
  "version": {{staffVersion}}
}

### 22. Deactivate them — re-run step 19 first to refresh @staffVersion — expect 204
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/staff/{{staffMembershipId}}/deactivate
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "version": {{staffVersion}}
}

### 23. Reactivate them — re-run step 19 first to refresh @staffVersion to the deactivated row's current version — expect 204
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/staff/{{staffMembershipId}}/reactivate
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "version": {{staffVersion}}
}

### 24. Change the current user's own password — expect 204, Set-Cookie __Host-tcm.session with a new stamp and no
### centre selected. Use the created staff member's "temporaryPassword" from step 20 as the current password if
### you log in as them instead; here the owner changes their own seeded password.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/change-password
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "currentPassword": "{{$dotenv password}}",
  "newPassword": "a-new-password-that-is-long-enough"
}

### Manual walkthrough of the audit log API (Day 32), continuing owner@nile.test's session from step 12. Re-run
### step 11/12 first if this session has ended (e.g. after step 24's password change).

### 25. List the audit log, filtered to membership changes — expect 200, { "items": [...], "nextCursor" }.
### The role change from step 21 should be the first item, with changes: [ { "field": "role", "before": "teacher", "after": "secretary" } ].
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/audit?entityType=membership

### 26. Page through the log one row at a time — expect 200 with exactly one item and a non-null "nextCursor".
# @name auditPage1
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/audit?pageSize=1

### 27. Fetch the next page — copy "nextCursor" from step 26 first — expect 200 with the next item.
@auditCursor = replace-with-the-nextCursor-from-step-26
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/audit?pageSize=1&cursor={{auditCursor}}

### Manual walkthrough of the centre settings API (Day 32), continuing owner@nile.test's session from step 12.

### 28. GET the centre's settings — expect 200, { "name", "slug", "timeZoneId", "defaultLocale", "version" }.
# @name centreSettings
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/centre/settings

### 29. Rename it and switch the default language — copy "version" from step 28 first — expect 204.
@centreVersion = replace-with-the-version-from-step-28
PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/centre/settings
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "name": "Nile Learning Centre",
  "defaultLocale": "en",
  "version": {{centreVersion}}
}

### 30. GET again — expect 200, the new name, "en", and a new version. /api/me (step 2) also now shows the new name.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/centre/settings

### Manual walkthrough of the students API (Day 41), continuing owner@nile.test's session from step 12.

### 31. List students — expect 200, { "items": [...], "nextCursor" }, empty or Nile-only.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/students

### 32. Register a student with a brand-new parent — expect 201, Location: /api/students/{newId}, body { "id", "parentId" }.
# @name registerStudent
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/students
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "fullName": "أحمد محمود",
  "phone": null,
  "schoolYear": "الصف الثالث الثانوي",
  "parent": { "fullName": "محمود حسن", "phone": "01012345678" },
  "relationship": "father"
}

### 33. Search for the student by a hamza-insensitive spelling of "أحمد" — expect 200, the student above in "items".
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/students?search=%D8%A7%D8%AD%D9%85%D8%AF

### 34. GET the created student via the Location header from step 32 — expect 200, one parent, isPrimary true.
@studentId = replace-with-the-id-from-step-32
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/students/{{studentId}}

### 35. Register a sibling using the existing-parent form — copy "parentId" from step 32 — expect 201, the same parentId back.
@firstParentId = replace-with-the-parentId-from-step-32
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/students
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "fullName": "محمد محمود",
  "phone": null,
  "schoolYear": null,
  "parent": { "id": "{{firstParentId}}" },
  "relationship": "father"
}

### 36. Update the student — copy "version" from step 34 first — expect 204.
@studentVersion = replace-with-the-version-from-step-34
PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/students/{{studentId}}
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "fullName": "أحمد محمود حسن",
  "phone": null,
  "schoolYear": "الصف الثالث الثانوي",
  "version": {{studentVersion}}
}

### 37. Link a second parent — re-run step 34 first to refresh @studentVersion, and register a second parent via
### step 32's pattern (a different phone) to get a @secondParentId first — expect 204.
@secondParentId = replace-with-a-second-parentId
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/students/{{studentId}}/parents
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "parentId": "{{secondParentId}}",
  "relationship": "mother",
  "version": {{studentVersion}}
}

### 38. Make the second parent primary — re-run step 34 first to refresh @studentVersion — expect 204.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/students/{{studentId}}/parents/{{secondParentId}}/make-primary
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "version": {{studentVersion}}
}

### 39. Unlink the first (now non-primary) parent — re-run step 34 first to refresh @studentVersion — expect 204.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/students/{{studentId}}/parents/{{firstParentId}}/unlink
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "version": {{studentVersion}}
}

### 40. Archive the student — re-run step 34 first to refresh @studentVersion — expect 204.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/students/{{studentId}}/archive
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "version": {{studentVersion}}
}

### 41. Restore it — re-run step 34 first to refresh @studentVersion to the archived row's current version — expect 204.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/students/{{studentId}}/restore
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "version": {{studentVersion}}
}

### Manual walkthrough of the groups API (Day 45), continuing owner@nile.test's session from step 12.

### 42. List groups — expect 200, { "items": [ { "id", "name", "subjectId", "subjectName", "teacherId", "teacherName",
### "capacity", "enrolledCount", "status", "version" } ] }: every Nile group, including the four seeded ones.
### Optional filters: ?subjectId=...&teacherId=...&includeArchived=true.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/groups

### 43. Create a group — copy a subject "id" from step 13 and a teacher "id" from GET /api/teachers first —
### expect 201, Location: /api/groups/{newId}, body { "id" }. Re-running it unchanged — expect 409 group.name_taken.
@groupSubjectId = replace-with-a-subject-id-from-step-13
@groupTeacherId = replace-with-a-teacher-id-from-GET-/api/teachers
# @name createGroup
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/groups
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "name": "فيزياء - المجموعة ج",
  "subjectId": "{{groupSubjectId}}",
  "teacherId": "{{groupTeacherId}}",
  "capacity": 20
}

### 44. GET the created group via the Location header — expect 200, status "active", enrolledCount 0.
GET {{baseUrl}}{{createGroup.response.headers.Location}}

### 45. Rename it — copy the "id" from step 43 and the "version" from step 44 below first — expect 204.
### Adding "subjectId" to this body (or to steps 46–47) — expect 400 request.malformed: the subject never changes.
@groupId = replace-with-the-id-from-step-43
@groupVersion = replace-with-the-version-from-step-44
PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/groups/{{groupId}}
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "name": "فيزياء - المجموعة د",
  "version": {{groupVersion}}
}

### 46. Change its capacity — re-run step 44 first to refresh @groupVersion — expect 204. Capacity 0 or 201 — expect
### 400 with errors.capacity.
PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/groups/{{groupId}}/capacity
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "capacity": 25,
  "version": {{groupVersion}}
}

### 47. Assign another teacher — re-run step 44 first, and set @groupNewTeacherId to another Nile teacher's id —
### expect 204. An archived teacher — expect 422 group.teacher_archived.
@groupNewTeacherId = replace-with-another-teacher-id
PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/groups/{{groupId}}/teacher
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "teacherId": "{{groupNewTeacherId}}",
  "version": {{groupVersion}}
}

### 48. Archive it — re-run step 44 first to refresh @groupVersion — expect 204; again — expect 422 group.already_archived.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/groups/{{groupId}}/archive
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "version": {{groupVersion}}
}

### 49. Restore it — re-run step 44 first to refresh @groupVersion — expect 204 (or 422 group.subject_archived /
### group.teacher_archived if its subject or teacher was archived in the meantime).
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/groups/{{groupId}}/restore
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "version": {{groupVersion}}
}

### 50. The ownership guard: log in as teacher@both.test and select nile-centre (steps 3–5, with the nile-centre
### centreId), then list groups — expect 200 with only the seeded group taught by the linked teacher record
### ("كريم فتحي"). GET /api/groups/{id} of any other group — expect 404 group.not_found, never 403. Any write
### (steps 43, 45–49) — expect 403 auth.permission_denied, even on the teacher's own group.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/groups